\title{easyDR: dose-response meta-analysis, argued like meta}
\description{
One call turns a table of exposure categories into a pooled dose-response.
easyDR is a front end for \code{\link[dosresmeta]{dosresmeta}}: that package
does the estimation, easyDR prepares the data, calls it once, and reports the
answer in the words a systematic reviewer uses.

The arguments are the ones a \code{meta} user already knows. There is no
\code{formula}. You write \code{sm = "RR"}, not \code{logrr ~ dose}.
}
\section{The family}{

\describe{
\item{\code{\link[=easydr]{easydr()}}}{fit it. One entry point for every outcome; \code{sm} decides
which columns are read.}
\item{\code{\link[=easydr_select]{easydr_select()}}}{compare candidate shapes, when you want the
comparison and are willing to read what it assumes.}
\item{\code{\link[=easydr_forest]{easydr_forest()}}}{the study trends and the pooled one, laid out as
a forest plot.}
\item{\code{\link[=easydr_curve]{easydr_curve()}}}{the fitted dose-response with its band.}
\item{\code{\link[=easydr_loo]{easydr_loo()}}}{leave one study out at a time.}
\item{\code{\link[=easydr_metareg]{easydr_metareg()}}}{does a study characteristic explain the
differences.}
\item{\code{\link[=easydr_bubble]{easydr_bubble()}}}{that meta-regression, drawn.}
\item{\code{\link[=easydr_funnel]{easydr_funnel()}}}{small-study effects, with Egger's test.}
}
